\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Invariant pairings and the two first-order operators}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{Give the \vocabk{based cochains}{fdip-00SH} a perfect
#{G}-invariant coefficient pairing #{b}. It may be symmetric
or alternating bilinear over #{k}, or Hermitian over a ring
with involution. In the Hermitian case take it conjugate-linear
in the first argument. Perfect means that the induced map to
the corresponding coefficient dual is an isomorphism. Define
##{\langle\alpha,\beta\rangle=
\sum_{x\in X}\sum_I b(\alpha_I(x),\beta_I(x)).}
Different exterior monomials are orthogonal.}

\p{Write #{(e_i\cdot)} for contraction by the #{i}-th dual basis
vector and set
##{\extdag_U=\sum_i(e_i\cdot)\bigl((T_i^U)^{-1}-\bbI\bigr),}
##{\extD_U=\extd_U+\extdag_U,\qquad
\cov_U=\extd_U-\extdag_U.}
These formulas define the operators algebraically; the next
lemma identifies #{\extdag_U} as the adjoint for the declared
pairing. In the earlier convention,
#{\covwedge_U=\extd_U} and #{\covdot_U=-\extdag_U}, as in
[the two sign conventions](fdip-00PV). Positivity is a separate
hypothesis, not part of this definition.}
